      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          NordicTrack sells two series of folding treadmills, and shoppers usually arrive at this
          comparison with a T Series 16 and a Commercial 1250 or 1750 open in two tabs, a few
          hundred dollars apart on sale and with screens that look much the same. The question is
          what the extra money buys. The short version is that it buys decline, a higher user
          rating on the 1250, and — on the 1750 and 2450 — a lifetime motor warranty in place of
          two years. Whether that matters depends almost entirely on whether you walk or run, and
          how often.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. NordicTrack T Series vs Commercial: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Choose the T Series if you mostly walk, incline-walk or run lightly a few times
            a week and want the lowest price into a 60-inch NordicTrack. Choose the Commercial
            series if you run regularly, want decline, or want NordicTrack's lifetime motor
            warranty — which means the 1750 or 2450, not the 1250.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            The <a href="/nordictrack-t-series-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>T Series 10</a> and 16 share a 20 by 60-inch belt, incline from 0 to 12% with no
            decline, a 12 mph top speed, a 325 lb maximum user weight, adjustable SelectFlex
            cushioning, the lowest step-up height in the range and a two-year motor warranty. The
            Commercial 1250, 1750 and 2450 add decline to -3%, RunFlex cushioning and, on the 1750
            and 2450, lifetime motor cover; the 1250's spec sheet lists a 400 lb user rating and
            the 2450 adds a 14 mph top speed and a confirmed 22-inch belt. Both series fold, both
            run iFIT, and both carry ten years on the frame.
          </p>
          <p>
            For most buyers deciding between the two, the Commercial 1750 is the better long-term
            buy, which is why it heads our
            <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ranking of the best NordicTrack treadmills</a>.
            But the T Series is a genuinely good fit for walkers, and paying for a running tier you
            will not use is not a virtue.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="side-by-side" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Side by Side: The Series-Level Figures</h2>
<p>
            The table carries only figures that NordicTrack, a retailer listing and independent
            coverage agree on, as set out in our range guide. Where sources conflict, the cell says
            so rather than giving a number.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Feature</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">T Series (10, 16)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Commercial (1250, 1750, 2450)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 x 60 in. confirmed on the 2450; 1250 and 1750 widths quoted inconsistently</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Incline</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0 to 12%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">-3% to 12%</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Top speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph (1250, 1750); 14 mph (2450)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Max user weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">325 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">400 lb on the 1250 spec sheet; 1750 quoted inconsistently</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Screen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 or 16 in., tilting</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 in. tilting (1250); 16 or 24 in. pivoting (1750, 2450)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Cushioning</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">SelectFlex, switchable</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">RunFlex</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Step-up height</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 8.3 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 10 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 years (1250); lifetime (1750, 2450)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frame warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 years</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">In-box weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 250 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 310 to 330 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folds</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two things stand out. First, the shared rows — 12% maximum incline, a 60-inch belt,
            folding, ten-year frame cover, iFIT — mean the series are closer than their names
            suggest. Second, the rows that differ are exactly the ones that decide how a treadmill
            copes with regular running: decline, user rating, belt width, and motor cover. The
            screen is the least important difference, even though it is the one the product pages
            lead with.
          </p>
          <p>
            Motor ratings are absent on purpose. NordicTrack and retailers quote the T Series 10's
            motor differently, and the <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Commercial 1250</a> and 1750's ratings have changed between
            model years; our
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1250-vs-1750/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Commercial 1250 vs 1750 comparison</a>
            sets out that conflict model by model. At series level, the motor warranty is a more
            reliable guide than any rating, and we come to it below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-each-is-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Each Series Is Designed For</h2>
<p>
            NordicTrack's three series are three different machines, not three sizes of the same
            one. Reading the two folding series as intended uses makes the choice clearer than
            reading them as good, better and best.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The T Series is a walking and light-running treadmill.</strong> Everything
            about it points that way. The step-up height is the lowest in the range, which helps
            anyone who finds stepping onto a deck awkward. The SelectFlex deck switches between a
            softer and a firmer feel, which suits walkers who want comfort and occasional joggers
            who want a little more response. The 0 to 12% incline covers the incline-walking
            sessions that are the most common use of a home treadmill. And the 60-inch belt means
            a jog does not feel cramped. What it does not offer — decline, a wider belt, a higher
            user rating, longer motor cover — are all things a walker can live without.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Commercial series is a running treadmill.</strong> The additions are the
            things that matter when you put more miles and more force through the deck. Decline to
            -3% lets iFIT route workouts include descents, and makes downhill training possible at
            all. The 1250's spec sheet lists a 400 lb user rating, against 325 lb on the T Series.
            The 2450's confirmed 22-inch belt gives a runner more room to drift without catching
            the side rail. And the 1750 and 2450 carry a lifetime motor warranty, which is
            NordicTrack's own statement that it expects those motors to stand up to heavy use.
          </p>
          <p>
            Neither series is the incline tier. Both stop at 12%, and if steep walking or hiking
            training is the point, NordicTrack's X Series incline trainers reach 40%. Our
            <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline trainer vs treadmill guide</a>
            covers whether that range is worth giving up folding for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Motor Warranty Is the Real Dividing Line</h2>
<p>
            If you take one figure from this comparison, take this one. NordicTrack covers the
            motor for two years on the T Series 10, the T Series 16 and the Commercial 1250, and
            for life on the Commercial 1750 and 2450 (and on both X Series machines). Frame cover
            is ten years across the range; parts are typically two years, and the screen and
            labour one year, everywhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            That split does not follow the series names. It runs through the middle of the
            Commercial series, which means "T Series vs Commercial" is really two questions: T
            Series vs Commercial 1250, where the motor cover is the same, and T Series vs
            Commercial 1750 or 2450, where it is not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why it matters more than a motor rating.</strong> Motor ratings are the most
            inconsistently quoted figure in NordicTrack's range, varying between the manufacturer,
            retailers and model years. A warranty term is a commitment: NordicTrack pays if the
            motor fails within it. Lifetime cover on the 1750 and 2450 is the clearest signal the
            company gives about which machines it expects to hold up under regular running, and
            it is the main reason the 1750 ranks above the cheaper models in our range guide.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it does not cover.</strong> A lifetime motor warranty is not a lifetime
            machine warranty. Parts on both series are typically two years, and labour one year,
            so a technician's visit to fit a covered motor in year four may still be at your
            expense, and a failed screen after the first year is yours to pay for. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to read component terms. Warranty terms also vary by retailer and model
            year, so confirm them for the unit you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-user-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Deck, Cushioning and User Weight</h2>
<p>
            Both series have a 60-inch belt, which is the length generally recommended for running
            because a running stride needs room to spare. The differences are in width, feel and
            rating.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Width.</strong> The T Series belt is 20 inches. The <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-2450-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Commercial 2450</a>'s is 22
            inches on figures every source agrees on; the 1250 and 1750's widths have been quoted
            differently across sources and model years, so check the listing for the unit you
            buy. Twenty inches is a common width and fine for most walkers. Two extra inches
            matters most to broad-shouldered runners, to anyone who drifts on the belt as they
            tire, and to people who like to walk with a slightly wider gait.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cushioning.</strong> The T Series uses SelectFlex, which switches between a
            softer and firmer setting; the Commercial series uses RunFlex. Cushioning names are
            marketing terms, and neither NordicTrack nor independent reviewers publish a
            measurement that would let us say one absorbs more than the other. What the switchable
            T Series deck does offer is a choice, which suits a household where one person walks
            and another jogs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>User weight.</strong> The T Series is rated to 325 lb. The 1250's spec sheet
            lists 400 lb; the 1750's rating is quoted inconsistently. A rating is a ceiling rather
            than a comfortable operating point: a machine used near its limit every day works its
            motor, rollers and belt harder than one used well within it. If the heaviest user in
            the house is above about 275 lb, the Commercial series is the safer choice, and above
            about 300 lb our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity treadmill guide</a>
            covers machines with more margin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-decline-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Incline, Decline and Speed</h2>
<p>
            On incline the two series are identical: both reach 12%. On decline and speed they are
            not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Decline.</strong> The Commercial series goes to -3%; the T Series does not
            decline at all. That has two uses. Inside iFIT, route workouts that follow a real
            course can reproduce its descents, which makes outdoor-route training feel closer to
            the real thing. Outside iFIT, a slight decline is useful for runners preparing for a
            hilly race and wanting to practise <a href="/what-is-treadmill-decline-good-for/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>downhill running</a>. If neither applies to you,
            decline is a nice-to-have rather than a reason to change series. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decline treadmill roundup</a>
            covers what decline is good for and the machines that go further than -3%.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Speed.</strong> Both T Series machines and the Commercial 1250 and 1750 top out
            at 12 mph, which is a five-minute mile. Very few home users ever need more. The
            Commercial 2450 is the exception at 14 mph, and it is the only folding NordicTrack
            that goes beyond 12. If you do interval work above 12 mph, it is the only choice in
            either series; if you do not, its extra speed is not worth paying for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline.</strong> Twelve per cent is enough for most incline-walking sessions
            and for <a href="/treadmill-hill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>hill repeats</a> in running. It is not enough for steep hiking training, and
            neither series can be made to go further. That is the X Series' job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="screen-and-ifit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Screens and iFIT: Less Different Than They Look</h2>
<p>
            Both series run iFIT on a built-in touchscreen, and iFIT can change speed and incline
            for you during classes on every current model. The free tier without a paid membership
            — manual mode plus a few template workouts — is described the same way by independent
            reviewers on both. So the platform does not separate the series.
          </p>
          <p>
            The screens differ in size and in how they move. The T Series 10 and Commercial 1250
            both have 10-inch screens that tilt. The T Series 16 has a 16-inch screen that tilts;
            the Commercial 1750 has a 16-inch screen that pivots towards the floor, which makes
            off-belt strength and mobility classes usable; the 2450 has a 24-inch pivoting screen.
            If you will do iFIT's floor classes, a pivoting screen is a real advantage. If you will
            only ever face forward and run, tilting is enough.
          </p>
          <p>
            The screen is also the component with the shortest warranty and the one that depends
            most on paying for a membership. Our guide to
            <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using a NordicTrack without iFIT</a>
            covers what each screen does when the membership stops. The practical rule from our
            range guide applies here too: choose the series on deck, incline and motor warranty
            first, then pick the screen you will actually use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-and-setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Space, Weight and Setup</h2>
<p>
            Both series fold, but they are not light, and the Commercial machines are heavier
            still.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delivery weight.</strong> NordicTrack lists in-box weights of about 250 lb for
            the T Series and about 310 to 330 lb for the Commercial series. Either needs at least
            two people and a clear route from the door; on stairs, the Commercial machines are a
            noticeably bigger job. Decide early whether to pay for assembly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step-up and ceiling.</strong> The T Series' step-up is about 8.3 inches and
            the Commercial series' about 10. Add your own height, a margin for the bounce of
            running and the lift of the deck at 12% incline, and check the total against the
            ceiling — particularly in a basement. The T Series' lower deck also helps anyone with
            limited mobility or balance.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Power.</strong> Treadmill motors draw a lot of current on start-up and under
            load, and manuals generally specify a dedicated grounded circuit for either series.
            Avoid extension leads and shared power strips; they cause faults that look like a
            failing machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-buys-which" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy Which</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy the T Series 10 if</strong> you mainly walk or incline-walk, jog
            occasionally, want iFIT on a built-in screen, and want to spend the least for a 60-inch
            NordicTrack. Everyone in the household should be comfortably under 325 lb.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the T Series 16 if</strong> you are a walker or light runner who will use
            iFIT classes most sessions and wants the bigger screen. But check the price against
            the Commercial 1750 on the day: on sale the two are often only a few hundred dollars
            apart, and for that difference the 1750 adds decline, a pivoting screen and lifetime
            motor cover.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the Commercial 1250 if</strong> you want decline and the higher listed user
            rating, mostly walk or run moderately, and are comfortable with two years of motor
            cover. It is the T Series 10's closest Commercial equivalent, with the same screen size
            and motor term.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the Commercial 1750 if</strong> you run several times a week, want a
            machine you expect to keep for many years, or are unsure — it is the model most
            NordicTrack buyers should start from, chiefly because of the lifetime motor warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the Commercial 2450 only if</strong> you will genuinely use speeds above 12
            mph or want the largest folding screen NordicTrack makes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy neither if</strong> you will never pay for iFIT, or if steep incline is the
            goal. For the first, a machine built without a platform is better value; for the
            second, look at the X Series. And if you are still weighing NordicTrack against its
            sister brand, our
            <a href="/nordictrack-vs-proform/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack vs ProForm comparison</a>
            explains where the two genuinely differ.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. If You Are Shopping: What to Check Before You Order</h2>
<p>
            Whichever series you settle on, four checks protect you from the most common
            NordicTrack <a href="/treadmill-buying-mistakes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>buying mistakes</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Confirm the model year.</strong> NordicTrack keeps names while changing
            screens, motor ratings and weight limits underneath them. Read the specification on
            NordicTrack's current page for the exact model number, not a review written about an
            earlier version, and keep a copy with your order.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Read the warranty by component.</strong> The motor term is the one that
            separates the 1750 and 2450 from everything cheaper. Get it in writing for the unit
            you are buying, particularly from a third-party retailer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Never pay list price.</strong> NordicTrack discounts its range almost
            constantly, and the gap between series moves with each promotion. Compare the actual
            prices on the day, not the list prices.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Budget for iFIT as an ongoing cost.</strong> Both series are built around it,
            and most of each screen depends on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the full current range ranked on those terms — including the X Series incline
            trainers and which older models to skip — see our
            <a href="/best-nordictrack-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best NordicTrack treadmills guide</a>,
            which carries the confirmed specification and the caveats for every model.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the NordicTrack T Series and Commercial series?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The T Series has a 20 by 60-inch belt, 0 to 12% incline with no decline, 12 mph, a 325 lb rating, switchable SelectFlex cushioning and two-year motor cover. The Commercial series adds decline to -3%, RunFlex cushioning, a 400 lb rating on the 1250’s spec sheet, 14 mph on the 2450, and lifetime motor cover on the 1750 and 2450.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the NordicTrack Commercial series worth it over the T Series?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For regular runners, usually yes — but specifically the 1750 or 2450, because they carry lifetime motor cover. The Commercial 1250 has the same two-year motor term as the T Series. For walkers and light joggers, the T Series does the job for less, and its lower step-up height is an advantage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the NordicTrack T Series have decline?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Both the T Series 10 and 16 incline from 0 to 12% and do not decline. The Commercial 1250, 1750 and 2450 decline to -3%, and the X Series incline trainers to -6%. Decline is most useful for iFIT route workouts that include descents and for runners preparing for hilly races.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which NordicTrack is better for running, T Series or Commercial?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Commercial series, and the 1750 in particular for most runners. It adds decline, a pivoting screen and a lifetime motor warranty. The 2450 is the only folding NordicTrack that reaches 14 mph. The T Series handles light, occasional running on its 60-inch belt, but it is designed primarily for walking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do the T Series and Commercial treadmills use the same iFIT?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Both run iFIT on a built-in touchscreen, iFIT can control speed and incline on both, and independent reviews describe the same free tier without a membership: manual mode and a few template workouts. The differences between the series are in the hardware and the warranty, not the software.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit on the NordicTrack T Series and Commercial?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The T Series 10 and 16 are rated to 325 lb on figures NordicTrack, a retailer and independent reviewers agree on. The Commercial 1250’s spec sheet lists 400 lb. The Commercial 1750’s maximum user weight has been quoted differently across sources and model years, so confirm it for the unit you are buying.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Choosing Between the Series</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come from reading NordicTrack’s series names as a simple good-better-best ladder.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every Commercial model has lifetime motor cover</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The lifetime motor warranty applies to the Commercial 1750 and 2450, not the 1250, which has two years — the same as the T Series. If long motor cover is your reason for moving up a series, the 1750 is the entry point, not the 1250.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on screen size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The T Series 16 and Commercial 1750 both have 16-inch screens, but only the 1750’s pivots, and the screen is the component with the shortest warranty. Decide on deck, decline, user rating and motor cover first, and treat the screen as the last decision.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a running tier you will not run on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Decline, a wider belt on the 2450 and 14 mph are real advantages for runners and largely irrelevant to walkers. If you mainly walk, the T Series’ lower step-up and switchable cushioning may suit you better than a Commercial machine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing list prices instead of sale prices</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">NordicTrack discounts constantly, and on sale the T Series 16 is often close to the Commercial 1750. Compare the prices on the day you buy; a few hundred dollars more can buy decline, a pivoting screen and lifetime motor cover.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

